    "evidence_text": "The conversations are fully transcribed and translated into Spanish.\n\nThe transcriptions also include annotations for code-switching and non-standard pronunciations.\n\nIntroduction\tRecent years have seen unprecedented progress for Natural Language Processing (NLP) on almost every NLP subtask.\n\nLow-resource languages still lag behind.\n\nEndangered languages pose an additional challenge.\n\nThus we applied standardized power normalization.\n\nThis is a standard procedure for speech recognition and synthesis datasets.\n\nexperiments, and one appropriate for multiple-speaker speech recognition and machine translation experiments.\n\nFor consistency, we use the same dataset splits for the machine translation experiments.\n\nThe statistics in Table reflect this split.\n\nThe potential applications of our resource, however, are not exhausted in language technologies.\n\nculture.\n\nWe used the same techniques here, as described above.\n\nThis gave\n\nus 13 hours and 48 minutes of speech.\n\nBaseline Results ::: Speech Recognition\tFor speech recognition\n\n(ASR) we used Kaldi .\n\nThe train/dev/test splits are across conversations, as described above.\n\nBaseline Results :::\n\nNLP for the under-represented languages of the Americas can be found at .\n\nWe benchmark NLP systems for speech synthesis, speech recognition, and machine translation, providing strong baseline results.",
